Assume a file-based system: everything worked on lives in one tree —
code, notes, rules, state.

Acting is select, then verb. The verbs close into a square:

            | read   | write
    content | o      | e
    tree    | ls f s | cp mv rm mkdir

One verb stands apart: run. To the machine it is the only one — the
machine never sees, finds, or edits; it runs a program that does. A
program is a file, so run turns a noun of the tree into a verb over
it — the verbs live in the tree too, and changing one is the same
square applied to it.

Every verb needs its noun, the selected thing — and each tool keeps
its own, a private "here": the shell its cwd, fm its cursor, ed its
file and line, the ai whatever was pasted at it last. Four heres,
none shared: every crossing between tools re-establishes place by
hand — retype, paste, walk again — and a key acts on a here that
scripts, history, and the other tools cannot see.

So the tree is shared but "this" is not. The system has proper nouns —
paths — and no pronoun.

The fix: give it one. cur, a file holding one address; c, the word that
says it — alone: print, with text: set to the address it starts with.
Verbs default their noun to it, and a bare destination lands beside
the noun. cur follows what the verb made — mv the new address, cp
the copy, mkdir the new directory; rm, making nothing, retreats to
the parent. Views read and write it instead of owning their own.

cwd is not it: per-process and dead with its shell, a container never
a thing, blind to a line. cd is stance; c is selection.

    you    keys    fm    ed    ai  speakers — anything that says a word
      \     |      |     |    /
          verbs                    the square, plus the pronoun
            |
      tree  +  cur                 data: every file. state: one file, here
            |
         screen                    every line an address
            |
         dispatch                  p — a shown address said back: retype,
                                   click, key

    c rule/general/canonical   this.
    c `{f | fzy}               by name, then this.
    c `{s -n canonical | fzy}  by content, then this.
    c `{m canonical}           by having been there, then this.
    fm's cursor, ed's save     a view, saying it by moving
    a click                    the screen, saying it back
                  |
                  v
                 cur
                  |
    o                          open it
    e                          edit it
    mv core                    rename it
    p                          dispatch it
    git add `{c}               anyone's — and that is the point

A find makes a noun and nothing else, so f at a prompt and f in a view
are one word and no verb is glued to either.

A verb's argument is a select — o path is c path, then o. One form, not
two, and the prefix is where the word was missing.

bin, re-read: p is dispatch as a word — handed shown text, it lands
it: file to o, file:line to e, url to the browser, commit to git
show. fze fzo fzs are the pronoun's absence paid in scripts —
o `{f | fzy} says fzo in parts, the middle kept in cur, not lost in
a variable — they retire. v stays, the tree over time in one letter.

cur is one address, now. Every address over time is m, cur's log —
alone: all of it, with n: n back. Back becomes a phrase, c `{m 1},
not a feature a view owns. Moving writes cur, nothing reads it until
a verb does. Selecting is not touching; select then verb is. So this
first — after it m's writer is one, before it three and all thrown
away.

Two hands, one pronoun — a second speaker can move this mid-verb. So
a verb resolves cur once, at its start: heard, the pronoun decays to
a proper noun and the sentence keeps it. The displaced find their old
this in m.

two-noun verbs — mv cp diff — still type their second noun. The day
that hurts, that: a second file beside cur, t its word, c's grammar.
mv alone is mv `{c} `{t}.

Three diffs to what exists: fm reads c at start and on focus,
writes it on every move; ed writes file:line on save; left click is c
on the word under the pointer. The fourth here costs nothing: the ai
is a speaker, not a view — the paste was its here because there was
nothing to ask, and now it asks c. Kill every view and the words still
work; bring one back and it stands where you were.
